{
  "image_name": "3b281688ad57cf3819348e33988b05f8.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes “cryptic sulfur metabolism” across very different environments, showing that sulfur is rapidly transformed through short-lived intermediates rather than simply accumulating as sulfate or sulfide. In marine oxygen-minimum zones, freshwater lakes, soils, hydrothermal vents, the atmosphere, and the human gut, sulfate reduction produces sulfide, which is then quickly re-oxidized—chemically or by microbes—into elemental sulfur, sulfite, thiosulfate, or back to sulfate. These linked oxidation–reduction loops appear tightly coupled to organic matter breakdown and other community metabolisms.\n\nAcross panels, the same core idea is repeated: sulfur compounds cycle simultaneously and closely, often across sharp oxygen gradients such as oxic waters, sediments, or mucus layers. Different organisms mediate parts of the loop, including sulfate-reducing microbes, sulfur-oxidizing bacteria, green and purple sulfur bacteria, and in the gut, taurine-reducing bacteria; some panels also note possible auxiliary roles for viruses. The atmospheric panel extends the concept to volatile sulfur gases like DMS and related oxidation products that contribute to aerosols and acid rain. Overall, the figure emphasizes sulfur cycling as a hidden but central process linking microbial activity, redox chemistry, nutrient turnover, and environmental or host conditions.",
